[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Every `/` is scanned as a comment; the operator branch is dead for `/`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tokenizer.c` `tokenizer_next_token()`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (c == '/' && (peek_char() == '/' || peek_char() == '*')) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     return scan_comment();
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `peek_char()` has not advanced, so it still returns `c`. The condition reduces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to `c == '/'`. Consequently `a / b` yields a `COMMENT` token `"/"`, `x /= y`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m yields `COMMENT "/"` followed by `OPERATOR "="`, and `/` never reaches
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scan_operator()`. A translation that "fixed" this to look at the *next*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m character would classify division as an operator and diverge on token type,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distribution counts and complexity score.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `src/tokenizer.rs::next_token` keeps the redundant `self.peek_char()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m test. Tests: `tokenizer_paths::slash_always_starts_a_comment_scan`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `token.column` is computed in `size_t` and truncated back to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tokenizer.c` `create_token()`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m token.column = current_column - token.length;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `current_column` is `int`, `token.length` is `size_t`, so the subtraction is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unsigned 64-bit and the result is truncated into an `int`. For a token at the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m start of a line this wraps negative — e.g. a 10-character identifier at column
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1 reports `C-9`. Printing `%d` shows the negative value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `current_column.wrapping_sub(length as i32)`, which agrees mod 2^32.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `tokenizer_paths::token_columns_wrap_negative_for_tokens_at_the_line_start`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. `analyze_text` throws away the newline tally it just computed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `analyzer.c`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m case TOKEN_NEWLINE: result.line_count++; break;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result.line_count = lines;   /* cumulative, from get_stats */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The per-run newline count is overwritten by the tokenizer's *cumulative*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `total_lines_processed`, which `tokenizer_reset()` deliberately does not clear.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So "Lines:" grows across successive analyses and includes lines seen by menu
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m choice 6 as well. Same for "Characters:".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `src/analyzer.rs::analyze_text` performs the same overwrite.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `menu_dispatch::analyze_accumulates_tokenizer_statistics_across_calls`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `file_paths::loading_a_file_twice_accumulates_statistics`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `boundaries::interleaved_analyses_keep_the_static_state_growing`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. `break` inside a `switch` does not leave the `while` loop
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.c` cases 2 and 5:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (!fgets(input, sizeof(input), stdin)) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     break;          /* leaves the switch, not the while(1) */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On EOF at the "Enter filename: " / "Enter pattern to search: " prompt the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program falls out of the switch and prints the whole menu again; only the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *next* `fgets` at the top of the loop ends it. The visible effect is one extra
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m menu block on stdout.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `if let Some(line) = stdin.fgets(256)` with no early return.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `file_paths::eof_at_the_filename_prompt_reprints_the_menu`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `analyzer_paths::pattern_search_eof_instead_of_a_pattern`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. A 255-byte input line silently terminates the input block
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fgets(line, 256, stdin)` reads at most 255 bytes. For a line of exactly 255
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m characters the newline is left in the stream, so the *next* `fgets` returns
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"\n"`, which `line[0] == '\n'` treats as the empty terminator line. The text
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that follows is consumed as menu input instead of as text.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `CStdin::fgets(256)` reproduces the 255-byte cap and newline retention.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `boundaries::a_255_byte_line_ends_the_input_block_early`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `boundaries::text_lines_around_the_fgets_buffer_size`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. Two size limits, three outcomes, for menu choice 2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `size > MAX_BUFFER_SIZE` (i.e. > 8192) → `read_file` prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Error: File too large` to stderr and returns NULL; no result block prints.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - exactly 8192 → `read_file` succeeds, then `tokenizer_load_text` rejects
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `length >= MAX_BUFFER_SIZE` with `Error: Input text too large`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `analyze_text` adds `Error: Failed to load text`. Both on stderr, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   **zeroed** result block still prints on stdout.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - ≤ 8191 → analyzed normally.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `file_paths::file_size_boundaries_around_max_buffer_size`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. `scan_string` / `scan_comment` can run one byte past their loop guard
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both loops guard on `length < MAX_TOKEN_LENGTH - 2` (254) but their two-byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m branches (`\\` + escaped char; `*` + `/`) append two bytes, so `length` reaches
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 256 and the C then writes `buffer[256]` on a `char buffer[256]` — a one-byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stack overrun. `create_token` afterwards clamps `length` to 255, so the token
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m text is unaffected.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `buffer[..length]` with `length` up to 256 on a `[u8; 256]` is in range,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `create_token` applies the same clamp, so no panic and identical output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `boundaries::escape_sequences_can_push_the_string_buffer_one_past_its_loop_guard`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `boundaries::block_comment_star_pair_at_the_buffer_edge`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. `sscanf("%d")` saturates then truncates
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc converts via `strtol` (clamping at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on overflow) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stores the low 32 bits into `int choice`. So `4294967297` selects menu item 1,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `4294967303` selects 7 (and exits), and `99999999999999999999999` becomes -1.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `cio::sscanf_int` saturates to `i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX` then casts `as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `menu_dispatch::choice_integer_conversion_truncates_the_way_the_c_does`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 9. Everything is a C string, so NUL bytes truncate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strncat`, `sscanf`, `strcspn`, `fopen`, `strstr` and the `content[read_size] =
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m '\0'` in `read_file` all treat their buffers as NUL-terminated. A NUL in stdin,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in a filename, in a search pattern, or in a loaded file discards the remainder.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `cio::cstr` is applied at each of those points.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `tokenizer_paths::nul_bytes_truncate_the_c_strings_they_appear_in`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `menu_dispatch::nul_byte_truncates_the_choice_buffer`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `file_paths::embedded_nul_truncates_the_file_contents`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `file_paths::filename_containing_a_nul_is_truncated`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `analyzer_paths::pattern_containing_a_nul_is_truncated`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 10. Bubble sort in `print_token_distribution` mutates the stored counts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The sort reorders `common_words`/`common_word_counts` in place, so the order
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m observed by a later call to choice 3 depends on the earlier call. It swaps only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m on strict `<`, so equal counts keep their relative order per pass.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: same in-place `swap` on the thread-local state.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `analyzer_paths::distribution_bubble_sort_ordering`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `analyzer_paths::word_tracking_accumulates_across_separate_analyses`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 11. `strncat` bound saturates the 4096-byte text buffer at 4095
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strncat(text, line, MAX_INPUT_SIZE - strlen(text) - 1)` reaches `n == 0` once
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strlen(text) == 4095`; further lines are read and discarded but the loop keeps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m running until an empty line or EOF.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `strncat_bounded` computes the same bound (and, importantly, the length
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m can never reach 4096, so the subtraction cannot underflow — confirmed by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m debug-profile run, which has overflow checks enabled).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tests: `boundaries::accumulated_text_around_max_input_size`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `boundaries::a_saturated_input_buffer_still_reads_and_discards_the_rest`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 12. `fopen` on a directory succeeds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `open(dir, O_RDONLY)` succeeds on Linux, so there is no "Could not open file"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m message; the subsequent `fread` fails with `EISDIR` and the contents come out
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m empty, which is then analyzed as an empty string.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `File::open` behaves the same and the read error is swallowed the way
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fread` returning 0 is. Tests: `file_paths::a_directory_opens_but_cannot_be_read`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 13. Unseekable inputs (checked, not asserted)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m For a FIFO or `/dev/stdin`, `fseek` fails and `ftell` returns -1; the C then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m calls `malloc(0)` and `fread(content, 1, (size_t)-1, file)`, which is undefined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviour. Both binaries were run against a named FIFO and `/dev/stdin` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produced identical stdout, stderr and status, but because the C path is UB this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is deliberately **not** encoded as a test assertion — its outcome is not a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m specification. `/proc/version` (stat size 0, but readable) *is* asserted, since
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that path is well defined: `ftell` gives 0 and nothing is read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Paths that no input can reach
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Enumerated for completeness; they are unreachable in the C, so there is nothing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to compare.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `tokenizer_peek_token()` and the `lookahead_valid` branch of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `tokenizer_next_token()` — `main.c` only ever calls `next_token`, `reset`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `load_text` and `get_stats`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `Error: Analyzer not initialized` — `main` calls `analyzer_init` before the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   loop and `initialized` is never cleared.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `find_patterns`' `!pattern` guard — the caller always passes a valid buffer.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `interactive_tokenizer`'s `Failed to load text` — its buffer is capped at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    212[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   4095 bytes, always under the 8192-byte limit `load_text` checks.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    213[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `Error: Memory allocation failed` — `malloc` of at most 8193 bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    214[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `TOKEN_WORD` (the `WORD` distribution row) and `TOKEN_WHITESPACE` — the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    215[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   tokenizer only ever emits `TOKEN_IDENTIFIER`, never `TOKEN_WORD`, and never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    216[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   emits a whitespace token.
